1. Difference between ArrayList and LinkedList in Java:
   - ArrayList is backed by a dynamic array, while LinkedList is backed by a doubly linked list.
   - ArrayList provides fast random access (O(1) time complexity) but slow insertions and deletions (O(n) time complexity) in the middle of the list. LinkedList provides slower random access (O(n) time complexity) but faster insertions and deletions (O(1) time complexity) at the beginning or end of the list.
   - ArrayList has a smaller memory footprint compared to LinkedList, as it only stores the elements, while LinkedList stores additional pointers for each element.
  2. When to use ArrayList vs LinkedList:
   - Use ArrayList when you need fast random access and don't expect to perform many insertions or deletions in the middle of the list.
   - Use LinkedList when you need to frequently add or remove elements from the beginning or end of the list, or when you need to perform many insertions and deletions in the middle of the list.

